RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
Office of City Clerk
March 23, 2009
The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne met on this date beginning at 6:00 p.m. in
Council Chambers.  Present were: MAYOR - Richard L. Kaysen; COUNCIL MEMBERS - Ms.
Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case, Mr. Collins, Mr. Pierson, Dr. Rinne and Mr. Spiker.
Absent: Mr. Valdez.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.

Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk; Barb Dorr, City Treasurer, and Dan White, City
Attorney.
Consent Agenda. (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to be
routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion.  There will be no separate
discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and support by two
other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be considered in its
normal sequence on the agenda.)  Upon request by Mr. Collins, and pursuant to support received by
Mr. Pierson and Mr. Brown, agenda item #27(h) was removed from the Consent Agenda.  Upon
request by Dr. Rinne, and pursuant to support received by Ms. Ash and Mr. Pierson, agenda item
#26(b) was removed from the Consent Agenda.  It was noted that procedurally members did not
need to officially provide a “second” to a request to remove consent agenda items. Mr. Collins
moved to approve remaining Consent Agenda items, seconded by Mr. Spiker.  Motion carried.
Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.
 [CA]
MINUTES FROM REGULAR MEETING OF THE GOVERNING BODY ON MARCH 9,
2009.  Motion (per Consent Agenda) carried.
 [CA]
MINUTES FROM VOUCHERS MEETING OF THE GOVERNING BODY ON MARCH
16, 2009.  Motion (per Consent Agenda) carried.
Mr. Collins moved to approve payment of vouchers, seconded by Mr. Brown.  City
Treasurer Barb Dorr advised, pursuant to the new Innoprise software program, she had distributed a
different voucher listing format that should be more user friendly, easier to read and more
descriptive.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of the governing body present.
Mayor Kaysen opened and conducted two public hearings for the purpose of receiving
comments and input on the following:
A public hearing to determine whether compliance of annexation conditions required by
Wyo. Stat. § 15-1-402 exists for the property described as being situated in Tracts 249, 250, 251,
252, Sunnyside Addition 6 Filing, except North 240 feet thereof and including portions of Charles
th
Street and Arthur Street.  Don Threewitt, Urban Planning Office, reviewed requirements and
compliance of conditions contained in Wyo. Stat. §15-1-402, including specific information
indicating compliance with Wyo. Stat. §15-1-402(a)(i) through (vi).  Ms. Broyles declared a
conflict of interest due to family ties with the applicant, and indicated she would not participate in
any discussion and vacated Council Chambers.  There being no further comments or input and
purpose of the public hearing having been met, Mayor Kaysen closed the hearing.  Following
closure of the hearing, Ms. Broyles returned to Council Chambers.
```

### Page 2 of 9

```text
A public hearing to determine whether compliance of annexation conditions required by
Wyo. Stat. § 15-1-402 exists for the property described as public road and alley right of way
situated as all of Campstool Road, Christensen Road, and Venture Drive as described in plats of
record for Cheyenne Business Parkway, Third filing and Venture Drive Addition.  Don Threewitt,
Urban Planning Office, reviewed requirements and compliance of conditions contained in Wyo.
Stat. §15-1-402, including specific information indicating compliance with Wyo. Stat. §15-1-
402(a)(i) through (vi).  There being no further comments or input and purpose of the public hearing
having been met, Mayor Kaysen closed the hearing
“AN ORDINANCE CREATING SECTION 10.24.170, DISTRACTIVE DRIVING
PROHIBITED; EXCEPTIONS, OF CHAPTER 10.24, RULES OF THE ROAD GENERALLY, OF
TITLE 10, VEHICLES AND TRAFFIC, OF THE CODE OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE,
WYOMING.”  Mayor Kaysen advised it was his understanding that a proposed amendment(s) is
affiliated with the ordinance and, upon no objection being raised by members of the governing
body, stated the public may wish to reserve their comments until the amendment(s) is offered.  In
conjunction with the most recent Public Services Committee meeting involving review and
recommendation on the proposed ordinance, Ms. Case requested the record reflect that pursuant to
Robert’s Rules of Order the Committee chair does not have to relinquish duties of the chair during a
Committee meeting to enable them to speak or vote on a Committee agenda item.  Reporting for
Public Services Committee, Dr. Rinne advised there was no recommendation on the item from
Committee.  Dr. Rinne moved  to approve (third and final reading), seconded by Mr. Brown.  Mr.
Brown moved to amend by substitute ordinance, seconded by Dr. Rinne.  It was advised the
proposed substitute (date stamped 3/17/09) removes references to distracted driver, changes
provisions of the ordinance to apply to cellular telephone use only, and provides that a violation
would constitute a primary offense. Comments in support as well as in opposition were offered by
the following individuals: John Fafoutakis, Eric Miller, Eric Russell, Marnie Sullivan, Landon
Brown, Holly Palmer, Donn Edmunds, Helen Munoz, John Palmer, Tom Lee, Ed Melko, Michelle
Schueler, Kathy Russell, Kevin Booker, Cindy Lang, Earl Janack, Marv Aragon, Jeffrey Smith and
Patty Mahelona. Topics discussed included: Government interference, regulation and enforcement
issues; preservation of individual rights; primary vs. secondary offense status; various types of
driving distractions, including text messaging; alternative solutions; notification of law, if approved,
for non-residents and tourism effect; hands-free cell phone usage; cell phone driver use involving
city, police and fire employees, and ambulance operators; safe driving habits, driver attention and
responsible driver issues; utilization of cell phones while driving as a privilege vs. a right; police
officer discretion when determining violations; opposition to the ordinance generally; common
sense driving practices; in-vehicle conversations as a form of driver distraction; statistical evidence
justifying cell phone use while driving as a primary offense; use of cellular phones while driving for
emergency, business, medical, employment, etc. purposes; current careless driving ordinance in
place; amendment suggestions; drivers’ freedom of choice; support for the ordinance; written
scientific research results by the Psychology Dept., University of Utah, on cell phone usage and risk
factors/accidents (information submitted and on file with office of City Clerk), and the citation
issuance process.  Upon inquiry, Police Chief Bob Fecht provided his definition of the term “legally
parked”, discussed officer discretionary authority, and advised the bond schedule for the offense
would be set by the Municipal Court Judge and fines assessed could be a maximum of $750.00
and/or 6 mos. in jail.  Upon further inquiry, Chief Fecht explained the citizen arrest (witness based)
process and proof of evidence. Discussion followed on the intent of the ordinance, which is to
provide safe driving practices; primary vs. secondary offense status; use of hands free devices;
```

### Page 3 of 9

```text
research on cell phone driver usage as a distraction; distractive driving legislation, and
constitutional issues.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of the governing body
present with the exception of Mr. Pierson voting “no”.  For the record, Mr. Pierson read a letter
from a citizen opposing the ordinance who was unable to be present at the meeting.  Discussion on
the main motion as amended included the following topics: Support/non-support for secondary
offense vs. primary offense status for violations; hindering law enforcement investigative operations
if the ordinance passes as a primary offense for violations; support for use of hands-free devices,
and current careless driving laws.  Main motion as amended by substitute failed (Clerk’s note:
Approval of ordinances on final reading require a minimum of six affirmative votes).  Voting “yes”
–  Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Mr. Collins and Mr. Spiker.  Voting “no” – Ms. Case, Mr.
Pierson, Dr. Rinne and Mayor Kaysen.  Under other City business later in the meeting, comments
were made concerning possible reconsideration of action on the ordinance at the next City Council
meeting and amending the offense for violations from a primary to secondary status.  Mr. Brown,
sponsor of the ordinance, stated as the purpose of the ordinance was to provide for safety of the
public, he would be against changing a violation from a primary to secondary offense.
Following a brief recess, Mayor Kaysen reconvened the meeting at 8:15 p.m.
“A RESOLUTION AUTHORIZING THE MAYOR AND THE CITY CLERK TO SIGN A
FINAL PLAT FOR SOUTH PARK ESTATES, 6TH FILING, A REPLAT OF A PORTION OF
SOUTH PARK ESTATES, 2ND FILING, INTERIOR HEIGHTS, STANFIELD ADDITION
BLOCKS 694 AND 744, STANFIELD 3RD ADDITION AND PORTIONS OF BLOCKS 713-714,
754-756, AND 775-777, ORIGINAL CITY, CHEYENNE, WYOMING (LOCATED NORTH OF I-
80, BETWEEN WALTERSCHEID BOULEVARD AND PARTOYAN DRIVE).” Kevin Keller,
agent and owner of the property, reviewed the voluntary remediation program involving the land,
and clarified outstanding issues that have been resolved regarding survey corrections, utility and
detention easements, and placement of the greenway (map handout submitted and on file with office
of City Clerk).  He discussed assessment of community development fees in the amount of
$21,890.00 vs. the greenway area to be provided and property valuation, and requested the City
purchase the greenway easement for the cost of the community development fees.  Reporting for
Public Services Committee, Mr. Brown moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Mr. Brown
moved to amend to include revised staff recommendation #5 to require placement of the greenway
easement to correspond with the most recent alignment proposed by Parks and Recreation Dept.,
seconded by Ms. Case.  Comments followed in support of the proposed greenway realignment path,
and proceeding with plat approval with the developer continuing negotiations with staff concerning
whether the City will purchase the greenway path land for the cost of the community facility fees.
Upon inquiry, City Attorney Dan White advised the governing body should act upon the final plat
separately from any agreement entered into with the developer, if desired, for the greenway land.
Upon inquiry, Jeff Wiggins, Parks and Recreation Dept., reviewed a similar past land transaction
situation involving the Pointe Park.  Martin Matsen, Development Director, advised there is no
lot/block identified on the plat for the greenway path and indicated if it is desired to use community
facility fees to buy back the area, a plat revision would be required as it would be treated as a
lot/block split.  It was noted there is no provision to waive community development fees in the
subdivision regulations.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Keller advised placement of the greenway easement on
the plat is not a requirement at the time of plat approval and the City could negotiate for the
easement at a later time, and indicated it was his intent to provide the greenway path easement in
exchange for payment in the amount of the community facility fees.  Discussion included housing
development growth in the area, past concessions approved through the Board of Adjustment,
```

