### Page 8 of 9

```text
[CA]
Consideration of Bid No. M-19-09 for a contract between the City of Cheyenne and Knife
River, Cheyenne, WY, for annual contract for furnishing aggregate for the City Street and Alley
Dept.  (2007-2010 1% Sales Tax).  Motion (per Consent Agenda) carried (amount not to exceed
$23,400.00).  (#5358)
Consideration of Bid No. M-20-09 for a contract between the City of Cheyenne and Knife
River, Cheyenne, WY, for annual contract for Hot Plant Mix, Asphalt Tack Oil and Cold Mix for
the City Street and Alley Dept. (2007-2010 1% Sales Tax).  Chris Olsen, STC Construction, voiced
concerns with the City doing its own overlay work and stated he believed it would be more cost-
effective for the City to contract with a private company, which would include a warranty period.
He discussed the proposed bid price for hot mix, costs involved for patching streets, street
deterioration, and quality of work issues, and suggested the City conduct a study on in-house vs.
private contractor costs.  During his comments, Mr. Olsen circulated photos depicting various street
conditions involving City overlay work.  Joe Stephenson, Knife River, provided information on the
cost of hot mix relative to the anticipated volume the City would utilize, and, upon inquiry, stated
the price for liquid asphalt is not dropping as it has for liquid oil.  He noted that the City has no cost
escalation clause in its contract.  Vicki Nemecek, Interim Public Works Director, reviewed City
equipment used for overlays/patching and the types of work performed by the City crew, and
explained timing issues involving quick response by the crews to address traffic and safety
concerns.  John Fafoutakis commented on the effect of oil prices, and the economic stimulus bill.
Mr. Spiker moved to approve the bid submitted by Knife River in an amount not to exceed
$654,200.00, seconded by Ms. Broyles.  Discussion included: Contracting with private businesses
vs. City in-house work and researching of cost comparisons, efficiencies and work issues prior to
the next annual bidding time frame; City’s crew providing overlays/patching in a timely manner,
volume of materials for projects, and ability of City crews to provide work on weekends as needed.
Upon inquiry, Ms. Nemecek reviewed the volume of mix utilized relative to overlays, scheduled
repairs, unanticipated immediate needs, and general maintenance work.  Motion carried.  Voting
“yes” – all members of the governing body present.  Mayor Kaysen stated a review of City
operations vs. services by private providers would be reviewed for efficiency and cost analysis.
(#5359)
